WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:06.160
This is the Technical Difficulties,
we’re playing Citation Needed.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.100
Joining me today, he reads books y'know,
it’s Chris Joel.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.100
Hello.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.280
Everybody’s favourite Gary Brannan,
Gary Brannan.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.660
♪ I’m in the old-fashioned bustle
my grandmother wore! ♪

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.720
He is, you know.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.090
And the bounciest man on the internet,
Matt Gray.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.080
Willkommen, YouTube!

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.919
In front of me, I’ve got an article from Wikipedia,
and these folks can’t see it.

00:00:28.919 --> 00:00:30.699
Every fact they get right is a point
and a ding

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.920
and there’s a special prize for particularly
good answers, which is…

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.120
And today, we are talking about
the Sark football team.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.080
Okay. Island off Jersey!

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.120
Yeah, have a point.

00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:44.179
Have a point for that straight away.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.920
- Do they play football?
- Is it about their... aagh!

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:47.940
Yeah, but American football.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.360
On a boat in Greenwich.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:51.720
- What?
- What?

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.080
Cutty Sark...

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:54.900
No?

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:55.720
Already?

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:56.400
Shall I leave?

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.400
Yes.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.860
Yes, Sark is part of the Bailiwick of Guernsey.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:01.870
Okay, fair enough.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.350
What are the Channel Islands?

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.140
Let’s start really, really far out.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.740
Are they... some islands...
in the English Channel, Tom?

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.440
Yeah, you’re not getting a point for that.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:13.520
- What?
- What?

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.600
You’re not getting a point
when I gave you the title!

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.000
I don’t understand the question, then.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.790
There’s something special about, sort of,
how they…

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.260
Oh, are you asking me what they’re called?

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:21.580
Oh, Jesus f***.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.540
There’s one called Jersey,
there’s one called Guernsey,

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.740
- Go on.
- ...and there’s one called St Helen’s?

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:27.740
No.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.520
- No.
- St Helen’s is near Wigan!

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.620
You’re thinking of St Helena, and that’s
on the other side of the planet.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:34.620
Okay, yes...

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:35.620
Alderney.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:36.620
Alderney is the other one, yes.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.380
Does one of them have a capital
of St Helena of Guernsey?

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:40.380
AUDIENCE MEMBER: Yes!

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.380
Yes.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.380
I’m getting something right.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:43.380
That’s my job!

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.520
And the thing is, you’re wrong,
it’s Saint Helier.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.580
When you say, “What are the Channel Islands?”

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.060
my standard answer is usually,
“Occupied France.”

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.920
Yes, and have a point.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:00.920
Absolutely, they were the only bit…

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.630
Did you just give a point for that?

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.320
Well, that’s one of the questions I was
going to ask.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.590
They were the only bit of the United Kingdom,
well, UK territory,

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.000
that was actually taken by Germany in the
Second World War.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.320
They were invaded in 1940, or ’41.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.480
I forget the year.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.460
I wasn’t there, why the f*** am I like that?
Honestly.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:20.320
In 1940, but in terms of Britain,
what are they?

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:21.330
Crown territory.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:22.330
Yes.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.260
Have the point, they are a crown dependency,

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.800
but they are not part of the United Kingdom.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.480
♪ Tax haven, wider than a mile... ♪

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.060
Oh, sorry, I thought you were
going for Goldfinger, there.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.760
- Same tune.
- They are the same song.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.580
And they are the same people that live there!

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:43.380
Is Sark the one where you don’t have any
motor vehicles?

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.220
Is it still bicycles and horses and carts?

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.420
Oh, yes, absolutely right.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.850
Sark is the one where cars are banned.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.800
Ladies and gentlemen, the Gary Brannan
General Knowledge Edition.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.760
The, “Gary’s mum and dad have been on
holiday to the Channel Islands,” edition,

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.490
and I sat through the photos.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.270
I need some slides...

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:04.810
So was that just, “And this is not a car,
and this is not a car, and this is not a car...

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:06.980
“This looks like a car, but in the back...

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.860
“very large hamster wheel.”

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.380
“This looks like a car.”

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.000
Pull up the bonnet: horse.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.100
Miniature horse?

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.100
Yes.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.140
Oh, cracking, want one.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.800
Isn’t Sark one where it is technically still
run by a lord?

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.720
- Feudal.
- Feudal, I think it is something like that.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.500
Oh, he’s getting all the points today, yes.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.730
He’s on home turf here, come on.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.400
Obscure crown territory facts, bring it on.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.800
It was considered the last feudal state in
Europe until 2008,

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.920
when they reformed it, but yes, that will...

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.150
You own the island, therefore
you own the people.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:36.420
Yeah, pretty much.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.410
Oh, please say they had a Communist revolution.

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:41.060
I know they didn’t but, you know?

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.100
- The horses.
- A horsey uprising!

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.560
'The Reform', it’s referred to...

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.790
That sounds more ominous than it
ought t'be, really, doesn’t it?

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:48.790
'The Reform.'

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.050
It is all capitalised.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.490
And if you didn’t like the idea,
would that be Sark snark?

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:54.490
Jesus.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:55.490
Yes?

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:56.940
No, it’s sark-asm.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.640
When you’re negative about it.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.890
Oh, guys, that deserved more,
sark-asm, it really did.

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:07.650
There are a lot of old laws still in place.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.560
They didn’t have divorce until 2003.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.250
Was it separation of bed and table?

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:13.819
I don’t know what that is.

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:17.310
That is the way you could organise a form
of divorce pre-divorce being allowed.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.900
You were allowed to live apart
by the church courts.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.370
Mensa et thoro.

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:23.840
Oh yeah, come on, it’s all in here somewhere!

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.300
I mean, I'm...

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.340
Because you’ve just basically told me the
entire first paragraph of a completely different

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.979
Wikipedia article I haven't loaded.

00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:33.979
So, yes.

00:04:33.979 --> 00:04:37.099
Third time in three shows, by the way.

00:04:37.099 --> 00:04:39.509
But how can you now get divorced on Sark?

00:04:39.509 --> 00:04:41.590
You can murder the other person.

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:42.900
Not technically a divorce.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.580
I reckon that was always an option,
you know, mate.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.480
Oh! Leave.
Get divorced, come back.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:48.420
Yes.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.960
You can now get divorced in Guernsey
and come back to Sark.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.080
“Those liberal Guernsey b******s,”
they probably think.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.229
Having been out on islands like that,

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:59.650
when I was on holidays a few years ago
I went out to one of the Hebridean islands.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:02.639
I went to Coll, which is a similar kind of thing,
very small island.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:07.180
They have a very slightly larger neighbour,
Tiree, who they see as being stuck up,

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:10.860
because A, they have the Co-op,
and B, they have the policeman.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:16.259
And what happens is when the policeman gets
on the boat to come over,

00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:20.759
they phone up the island, and everyone hides
their non-registered cars and stuff

00:05:20.759 --> 00:05:23.330
until the policeman has left.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:26.789
And now you’ve just ratted all of them out.

00:05:26.789 --> 00:05:29.060
Yeah! Get over there,
policeman from Tiree, come on!

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:31.200
But by the time he’s got there, they’ll
have hidden them again.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:32.560
Hidden the cars, yes.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.560
Somewhere on that island, the phone's just...

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.400
“Oh, we said not to tell anyone!”

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.300
“Gary, we told you...!”

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.240
We briefly mentioned the economy, what is
Sark’s economy driven by?

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.700
Horses!

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:47.080
Sarcasm.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:48.380
Tourism.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.580
Yes, and you also said that earlier,
financial services.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:55.280
Eh, kids?

00:05:55.289 --> 00:06:00.500
It has... a low amount of tax,
let’s say that.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:01.669
How long do you have to live there?

00:06:01.669 --> 00:06:04.020
It’s got to be over a number of years, probably.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:09.040
I’m guessing owning a tiny, tiny, tiny, tiny
fraction of a bit of land counts as living there.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.919
Three months in a tax year.

00:06:10.919 --> 00:06:11.919
Okay, fair enough.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:13.180
If you’re there for 91 days, you’re good.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:14.600
You’re a resident,

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.620
you get their tax laws if you want them.

00:06:16.629 --> 00:06:18.361
Don’t do this, I’m really crap at tax stuff.

00:06:18.361 --> 00:06:19.361
Do they have to be...

00:06:19.361 --> 00:06:20.500
I’m rapidly figuring out if I can do this,

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:22.870
the answer is almost certainly no,
but I’m thick as mince.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:24.360
Don’t let me near this.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:25.710
Just a sec.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:29.719
This means all your tax savings could be spent
on getting a hovercraft there,

00:06:29.719 --> 00:06:30.930
and then you could get a hovercraft there,

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:32.280
and that would be fun, because it’s a hovercraft.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:34.900
It is quite a long way away.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:37.279
Tom, Tom, Tom, please can I have
a go on a hovercraft?

00:06:37.279 --> 00:06:40.089
What we’re saying is we all want a go on
a hovercraft.

00:06:40.089 --> 00:06:43.380
And I think I can arrange that, but...

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:44.380
Ooh!

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:45.960
Smash cut to...

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.569
I’m more worried about Matt’s tax advice
system here, which is

00:06:50.569 --> 00:06:53.820
- “Yes, we can save you money for a hovercraft.”
- Hovercraft, hovercraft, hovercraft.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:58.939
Like, it’s not the worst tax advice
I’ve ever heard, but…

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:02.260
If we did, we could do a show on a hovercraft,
it would be ‘on air’, because it’s…

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:08.320
It’s a golf clap. It’s a golf clap.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:09.360
It's a good four.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.420
I’m not biscuiting that.

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:13.680
The legal system on Sark
also has something called...

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.680
er, I’m going to try and pronounce French
again here, which is never great.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:17.680
Oh great.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.930
The Clameur de haro.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:20.930
Clammy arrows?

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:24.009
Is that along the lines of the hue and cry
for a criminal, or something like that?

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:25.509
Oh, it’s connected to hue and cry.

00:07:25.509 --> 00:07:27.300
I’ll absolutely give you a point for that.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.249
Yes, for apprehending a felon,
or something like that, no?

00:07:29.249 --> 00:07:31.600
Ah, not in this case, but
what is the hue and cry?

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.189
Hue and cry is an ancient thing where in a
community

00:07:34.189 --> 00:07:37.339
you would be responsible for raising the hue
and cry if someone had committed a crime

00:07:37.339 --> 00:07:38.720
and was passing through your community.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:41.610
It’s, “All pile on,” basically, in a
legal term.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.729
Right, so it's like the law,

00:07:43.729 --> 00:07:47.360
but on a small island where the law operates
like American football.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:49.960
This is not on Sark.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:52.419
I should point out for a hue and cry,

00:07:52.419 --> 00:07:56.449
all able-bodied men, upon hearing the shouts,
were obliged to assist.

00:07:56.449 --> 00:07:58.780
Oh, that sounds like so much fun.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.500
This is the hue and cry, though, this is not
Clameur de haro.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.140
A-r-r-o-w?

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:06.580
No, h-a-r-o.

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:07.580
Oh.

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.039
This is kind of the opposite.

00:08:09.039 --> 00:08:11.130
This is not going to catch someone,
this is to stop someone.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:12.130
Ignore someone.

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:14.550
I was going to say, professionally ignoring
crimes.

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:16.580
Wait, tax haven!

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:20.020
“Hovercraft, what hovercraft?”

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:21.849
“It’s disguised as a barn, officer!”

00:08:21.849 --> 00:08:24.860
No, it’s disguised as a load of money.

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:27.620
Is that a formal way of saying,

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:29.120
“Geroff my land!”?

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.140
Yeah, go on.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:39.680
It’s not, “Get off my land,” but yes,
it is a formal Thing that you Do.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:41.650
This is annoying me, because I half know this.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:44.300
It’s some kind of judicial process where

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.620
you effectively bring someone
in front of the king

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:48.720
to adjudge on a land case or
something like that, isn’t it?

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:50.940
It is, it's a very specific process.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:54.300
The procedure is performed on one’s knees.

00:08:56.889 --> 00:08:58.780
Steady everyone.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:00.980
Before at least two witnesses,

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.060
in the presence of the wrongdoer,

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.760
and in the location of the offence.

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:13.700
All right?

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.490
The Criant, the person complaining,

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.920
with his hand in the air must call out,

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:20.779
“Hear me, hear me, hear me.

00:09:20.779 --> 00:09:23.170
“Come to my aid, my prince,
for someone does me wrong.”

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.040
- Yes.
- That’s a hell of a safe word.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:29.670
Try speaking that through the orange, yeah!

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:33.990
Followed by reciting what well-known thing
in French?

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:35.060
Lord’s prayer.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:36.060
Yes, absolutely right.

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:37.060
It’s been done recently, this.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:38.480
- Yes it has.
- This is why I’ve heard of it,

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.240
it’s because it’s in
some kind of land dispute,

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:43.400
and it’s something like a hedge or a garage
or something like that,

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:46.680
and the guy is basically on the verge of 
losing the case,

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:51.029
and as one final, basically, legal dick move
just dropped to his knees and did that.

00:09:51.029 --> 00:09:52.850
And everyone went,
“Oh, s***, that’s still enforced.”

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:53.850
That kind of thing.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.149
What happens after the
Lord’s prayer is recited?

00:09:57.149 --> 00:10:00.540
What does the person they are challenging
need to do?

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:03.410
Based on what’s already gone before, presumably
adopt a backwards crab position,

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:06.370
walk in a circle on a full moon,

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:10.660
but any other time of the year, you have to
go completely rectilinear,

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:14.290
and shout something, yeah,
let’s go with Latin.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:16.440
It is significantly simpler than that.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:18.020
Most things are!

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:22.760
There are people in this room with doctoral
theses that were easier to complete than that.

00:10:22.769 --> 00:10:25.500
Do they just go, “Right”?

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:28.250
Yes, that’s basically it.

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:31.690
They just have to stop what they’re doing.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:35.500
Regardless of whether they are legally entitled
to do it or not,

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:38.680
if that is used they have to stop
and it goes to adjudication.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.400
Wouldn’t you if someone got on their knees
and did all of that?

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:42.400
Yes, to be fair, yes.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:43.540
Yeah, but only to watch!

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:46.449
As the bulldozer rumbled towards them, yes.

00:10:46.449 --> 00:10:49.850
And if you call without a valid reason,
you pay a fine.

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:50.850
If you call and, er...

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:52.110
